  "original_native_alert": {
    "item": "thm-birkhoff-limit-identification-on-finite-measure-spaces",
    "finding": "Proof 1.1 defines g* as the pointwise limit on the convergence set and zero elsewhere. Proof 2.1 then says: \u201cFor \u03b5>0, every point of D_{m,k} belongs to the positive-maximal set of (g\u2212(k/m\u2212\u03b5))1_{D_{m,k}}.\u201d The universal pointwise assertion need not hold on the exceptional null set assigned g*=0. For example, take X={p} disjoint union {1,2,...}, give p probability one and every integer probability zero, set T(p)=p, T(j)=j+1, g(p)=0 and g(j)=\u2212j\u00b2. This is a finite measure-preserving system with integrable finite-valued g. The averages diverge to \u2212\u221e on every integer, so the prescribed g* is zero everywhere and D_{m,0}=X. With \u03b5=1/2, every partial sum of g+\u03b5 starting at an integer is negative, so those points are outside the positive-maximal set. The missing qualification concerns only a null set and does not undermine the integral conclusion.",
    "severity": "gap-a-reader-closes"
  },
