  "confirmed_counterexample": "X={p} union positive integers with mass1 atp and mass0 at every integer; T fixesp and sendsj toj+1; g(p)=0,g(j)=-j\u00b2. All maps/functions measurable in the power-set sigma-algebra, T preserves the measure, g is finite valued and integrable with integral0. On every integer A_ng diverges to -infinity, hence assigned g*=0 gives D_m,0=X, but every S_n(g+1/2) there is negative. The old universal point assertion fails while the integral conclusion survives. The repaired M omits only null integers.",
  "independent_current_review": {
    "convergence": "The exact shifted-average identity and rearrangement prove x\u2208C iff Tx\u2208C and equal finite limits. C is measurable conull by actual Birkhoff theorem. The zero extension is strictly invariant; no completeness or invertibility is used.",
    "maximal": "The actual maximal theorem applies to finite-valued real integrable q and gives \u222bM q\u22650 on arbitrary measure spaces. Outside strict-invariant D, every iterate lies outsideD and all sums vanish. On D\u2229C, the averages tend to at least epsilon. The explicit null replacement is legitimate for signed q because both parts have zero null-set integral.",
    "summation": "Both g and g* are integrable, so countable stratum summation is legitimate via positive/negative finite masses. Finite total measure makes the \u03bcX/m error vanish; applying the bound to -g gives equality.",
    "identification": "Strict-invariant event multiplication yields the event identities. The finite signed indefinite integral has finite total variation, absolute continuity and the single-set finite exhaustion required by the actual RN theorem under AC. Rational superlevel comparisons and strict representatives identify the RN density with f* a.e.; complex components and \u03bcX=0 remain covered.",
